1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,478
At the Corner Cafe, the iced tea sold out
early on the last hot afternoon.

2
00:00:05,498 --> 00:00:10,088
A weather service could have warned Asha,
but it answers in JSON.

3
00:00:10,108 --> 00:00:12,428
And her quiz is data inside data.

4
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:15,344
This is Code Quest, Chapter 23.

5
00:00:15,364 --> 00:00:18,670
Today, your programs talk to other
programs.

6
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:23,986
And an AI assistant will read a supplier's
price list, and promise you a number.

7
00:00:24,006 --> 00:00:25,511
Your tests will decide.

8
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:31,450
Today's mission: a daily board that reads
a weather response, and picks the drink of

9
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:31,960
the day.

10
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,900
Then it saves the board as JSON, for the
screen behind the counter.

11
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:39,176
Let's build it, piece by piece.

12
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:44,090
Turn data into JSON text and back, and
save it in files.

13
00:00:44,133 --> 00:00:48,463
Explain how a web service answers, and
read the values you need.

14
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:52,591
And ask an AI to explain a response, then
check its code.

15
00:00:53,183 --> 00:00:58,821
JSON, short for JavaScript Object
Notation, is a text format for structured

16
00:00:58,841 --> 00:01:03,573
data: dictionaries, lists, text and
numbers, inside each other.

17
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:05,907
Almost every language can read it.

18
00:01:05,927 --> 00:01:10,151
So many weather, payment and delivery
services answer in JSON.

19
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:14,873
JSON looks almost like a Python
dictionary, but it is text.

20
00:01:14,893 --> 00:01:17,330
A JSON object becomes a dictionary.

21
00:01:17,367 --> 00:01:19,107
An array becomes a list.

22
00:01:19,133 --> 00:01:21,268
A string becomes a string.

23
00:01:21,288 --> 00:01:24,583
JSON allows only double quotation marks.

24
00:01:24,633 --> 00:01:27,623
True and false are written in small
letters.

25
00:01:27,643 --> 00:01:30,086
And null is JSON's word for nothing.

26
00:01:30,687 --> 00:01:34,418
The json module translates in both
directions.

27
00:01:34,438 --> 00:01:37,680
Dump s turns Python data into JSON text.

28
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:40,697
Load s turns the text back into data.

29
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:42,325
Read the S as string.

30
00:01:42,345 --> 00:01:46,470
Without the S, dump and load work with a
file instead.

31
00:01:47,087 --> 00:01:51,957
A web API is a service that other programs
can ask for data.

32
00:01:51,977 --> 00:01:55,707
Your program sends a request to a web
address.

33
00:01:55,733 --> 00:01:57,901
The service sends back a response.

34
00:01:57,921 --> 00:02:02,533
The status code says how it went: 200
means OK, and 404 means not found.

35
00:02:02,567 --> 00:02:05,655
The body holds the data, usually as JSON.

36
00:02:05,675 --> 00:02:10,735
Many services also want an API key: a
secret password for programs.

37
00:02:10,755 --> 00:02:14,223
Keep it out of your code, and out of AI
chats.

38
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:15,636
Quick check.

39
00:02:15,656 --> 00:02:18,213
What does JSON null become in Python?

40
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:29,708
D.

41
00:02:29,728 --> 00:02:30,077
None.

42
00:02:30,097 --> 00:02:32,883
Null and None both mean: nothing here.

43
00:02:33,487 --> 00:02:34,084
Breather.

44
00:02:34,104 --> 00:02:37,447
You know what JSON is, and how a service
answers.

45
00:02:37,467 --> 00:02:41,126
Next, Asha turns her first dictionary into
JSON.

46
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:46,382
Step 1.

47
00:02:46,402 --> 00:02:50,878
Lines 4 and 5 hold a Python dictionary for
one menu item.

48
00:02:50,898 --> 00:02:54,270
It holds a string, a float, True, and
None.

49
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:58,047
Line 6 turns it into JSON text with dump
s.

50
00:02:58,067 --> 00:03:01,989
Line 7 prints it, and line 8 prints its
type.

51
00:03:13,347 --> 00:03:16,052
True becomes true, in small letters.

52
00:03:16,072 --> 00:03:17,718
And None becomes null.

53
00:03:17,738 --> 00:03:19,687
Exactly as the table said.

54
00:03:19,733 --> 00:03:21,622
And the result is a string.

55
00:03:21,642 --> 00:03:26,004
Now Asha can save it in a file, or send it
to another program.

56
00:03:26,583 --> 00:03:27,147
Step 2.

57
00:03:27,167 --> 00:03:30,232
Here is a saved file, scone dot json.

58
00:03:30,252 --> 00:03:33,816
Look at the sizes: an array of two
strings.

59
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:36,687
Lines 5 and 6 read the file.

60
00:03:36,707 --> 00:03:38,729
Read text gives a string.

61
00:03:38,749 --> 00:03:42,650
Line 7 turns that string into data, with
load s.

62
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:44,632
Line 8 prints both types.

63
00:03:44,652 --> 00:03:47,443
Line 9 picks one size from the list.

64
00:03:58,813 --> 00:03:59,246
Large.

65
00:03:59,266 --> 00:04:05,353
The text became a dictionary, so sizes is
a list, and index 1 is its second item.

66
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:09,200
Line 10 shows false and null as Python's
False and None.

67
00:04:09,233 --> 00:04:13,032
To check for None, write is None, as line
11 does.

68
00:04:13,052 --> 00:04:17,308
Python's style guide, pep eight,
recommends is for None.

69
00:04:17,883 --> 00:04:18,439
Step 3.

70
00:04:18,459 --> 00:04:22,553
Line 4 holds a drink with an accent: Café
au lait.

71
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:24,920
Lines 6 and 7 add two settings.

72
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:28,997
Ensure ascii equals False keeps every
letter as it is.

73
00:04:29,017 --> 00:04:32,620
Indent equals 2 puts each value on its own
line.

74
00:04:32,667 --> 00:04:34,428
Line 5 has no settings.

75
00:04:34,448 --> 00:04:37,138
Look closely at the accent in Café.

76
00:04:48,513 --> 00:04:51,928
As a code: C a f, then backslash u 0 0 e
9.

77
00:04:51,948 --> 00:04:57,839
By default, dump s writes every character
outside basic English as a code.

78
00:04:57,859 --> 00:05:01,353
The data is still correct, but hard to
read.

79
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:03,775
Line 6 keeps the real letter.

80
00:05:03,795 --> 00:05:07,656
Line 7 also indents, two spaces for each
level.

81
00:05:08,250 --> 00:05:08,753
Step 4.

82
00:05:08,773 --> 00:05:14,287
Here is Asha's quiz: a title, a pass mark,
and a list of questions, each a

83
00:05:14,307 --> 00:05:15,110
dictionary.

84
00:05:15,133 --> 00:05:17,581
Line 12 uses dump, without the S.

85
00:05:17,601 --> 00:05:20,423
It writes straight into the open file.

86
00:05:20,467 --> 00:05:23,035
Line 15 uses load, without the S.

87
00:05:23,055 --> 00:05:25,937
It reads the data back from the file.

88
00:05:25,967 --> 00:05:29,216
Line 20 compares the loaded data with the
original.

89
00:05:40,580 --> 00:05:40,894
True.

90
00:05:40,914 --> 00:05:45,040
The loaded quiz is equal to the original,
so nothing was lost.

91
00:05:45,067 --> 00:05:49,868
Line 19 reaches inside: the second
question, then its ask.

92
00:05:50,487 --> 00:05:51,143
Breather.

93
00:05:51,163 --> 00:05:54,902
Asha's quiz now lives in a file, data
inside data.

94
00:05:54,922 --> 00:05:56,857
Next, the weather service.

95
00:06:01,583 --> 00:06:02,036
Step 5.

96
00:06:02,056 --> 00:06:06,356
On your own computer, a program can ask a
real service for data.

97
00:06:06,376 --> 00:06:10,743
The url lib module, from the standard
library, sends the request.

98
00:06:10,767 --> 00:06:17,623
An error status, such as 404, makes url
open raise an H T T P error, which try can

99
00:06:17,643 --> 00:06:18,127
catch.

100
00:06:18,167 --> 00:06:20,161
This address is a placeholder.

101
00:06:20,181 --> 00:06:25,264
Live answers change every minute, so
programmers save one real response as a

102
00:06:25,284 --> 00:06:28,420
file, called a sample response, and test
on it.

103
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:30,638
Here is Asha's sample.

104
00:06:30,658 --> 00:06:33,094
Location and current are objects.

105
00:06:33,114 --> 00:06:35,774
Forecast is a list of three objects.

106
00:06:36,387 --> 00:06:40,147
Lines 5 and 6 read the file, and turn it
into data.

107
00:06:40,167 --> 00:06:43,095
Follow the path from the outside in.

108
00:06:43,115 --> 00:06:45,797
Line 7 opens location, then name.

109
00:06:45,817 --> 00:06:50,383
Line 8 opens current, then temp C, the
number inside it.

110
00:07:01,747 --> 00:07:02,952
Riverside 31.5.

111
00:07:02,972 --> 00:07:07,607
The name from location, and the
temperature from current.

112
00:07:07,633 --> 00:07:11,800
Forecast is a list, so lines 10 and 11
loop over it.

113
00:07:11,820 --> 00:07:13,893
Each slot is a dictionary.

114
00:07:14,483 --> 00:07:14,971
Step 6.

115
00:07:14,991 --> 00:07:17,292
A service can leave a field out.

116
00:07:17,312 --> 00:07:21,643
Get, from Chapter 12, returns a default
instead of crashing.

117
00:07:21,667 --> 00:07:23,948
The sample has no wind field.

118
00:07:23,968 --> 00:07:26,169
Line 9 asks for it with get.

119
00:07:26,189 --> 00:07:28,867
Line 10 asks with square brackets.

120
00:07:40,247 --> 00:07:43,717
Line 10 stops with a key error: wind k p
h.

121
00:07:43,737 --> 00:07:47,207
Square brackets need the field to be
there.

122
00:07:47,233 --> 00:07:49,621
Line 9 printed the default instead.

123
00:07:49,641 --> 00:07:52,303
Use get for fields that may be missing.

124
00:07:52,920 --> 00:07:57,397
Text that is not valid JSON raises a JSON
decode error.

125
00:07:57,417 --> 00:07:59,441
Line 4 holds three texts.

126
00:07:59,461 --> 00:08:01,730
Look at the quotation marks.

127
00:08:01,767 --> 00:08:06,719
Lines 6 to 9 catch the error with try, as
in Chapter 22.

128
00:08:06,739 --> 00:08:10,005
Error dot msg holds the short reason.

129
00:08:21,380 --> 00:08:22,521
Only the first.

130
00:08:22,541 --> 00:08:27,240
JSON allows only double quotation marks,
so the second fails.

131
00:08:27,267 --> 00:08:28,704
The third is empty.

132
00:08:28,724 --> 00:08:33,228
That happens when a service sends no body:
expecting value.

133
00:08:33,820 --> 00:08:34,603
Quick check.

134
00:08:34,623 --> 00:08:38,216
Which function writes JSON straight into
an open file?

135
00:08:49,580 --> 00:08:49,717
A.

136
00:08:49,737 --> 00:08:51,289
Dump, without the S.

137
00:08:51,309 --> 00:08:53,803
Dump s returns a string instead.

138
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:55,003
Breather.

139
00:08:55,023 --> 00:08:56,679
Now, back to the mission.

140
00:09:01,417 --> 00:09:02,839
Back to the mission.

141
00:09:02,859 --> 00:09:07,597
Lines 6 to 8 are a small function: open a
file, and load its JSON.

142
00:09:07,633 --> 00:09:10,921
Line 12 follows the path to the
temperature.

143
00:09:10,941 --> 00:09:14,303
Line 13 picks iced tea at 30 degrees or
more.

144
00:09:14,333 --> 00:09:21,283
Lines 14 and 15 keep the hour of every
slot with a rain chance of 30 or more.

145
00:09:21,333 --> 00:09:23,514
Lines 16 and 17 build the board.

146
00:09:23,534 --> 00:09:28,120
Lines 19 to 21 save it as JSON, with an
accent, and print the file.

147
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:33,756
Remember the sample's forecast: three
slots, each with its own rain chance.

148
00:09:45,113 --> 00:09:45,960
Only 18:00.

149
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:47,694
Its rain chance is 40.

150
00:09:47,714 --> 00:09:50,373
The other two slots have 0 and 10.

151
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:57,650
It reads JSON, picks what it needs, and
writes new JSON for the cafe's screen.

152
00:09:57,700 --> 00:10:03,593
Next, Asha reads her milk supplier's
prices, with an AI assistant, one stage at

153
00:10:03,613 --> 00:10:04,117
a time.

154
00:10:04,717 --> 00:10:10,687
This chapter's AI skill: paste a sample
response, ask the AI to explain it, then

155
00:10:10,707 --> 00:10:14,207
check every field name against the real
sample.

156
00:10:14,233 --> 00:10:18,292
And never paste an API key, or private
customer data.

157
00:10:18,887 --> 00:10:22,913
Here is the supplier's saved response,
prices dot json.

158
00:10:22,933 --> 00:10:24,899
Look closely at each price.

159
00:10:25,520 --> 00:10:26,554
Stage 1: plan.

160
00:10:26,574 --> 00:10:31,597
Asha pastes the sample, and asks for its
structure, level by level.

161
00:10:31,617 --> 00:10:32,500
No code yet.

162
00:10:32,533 --> 00:10:38,095
The AI says items is a list, and every
price is text in quotation marks.

163
00:10:38,115 --> 00:10:41,040
Asha checks the sample: both are true.

164
00:10:41,653 --> 00:10:42,650
Stage 2: write.

165
00:10:42,670 --> 00:10:45,021
Asha writes her own price of first.

166
00:10:45,041 --> 00:10:48,883
It loops over the list, and converts the
text with float.

167
00:10:48,933 --> 00:10:54,333
The AI says milk gives 1.2, and line 10
handles a missing item.

168
00:10:54,367 --> 00:10:55,301
She runs it.

169
00:10:55,321 --> 00:10:58,243
Milk gives 1.2, and flour gives None.

170
00:10:58,263 --> 00:10:59,675
Her version works.

171
00:11:00,287 --> 00:11:01,668
Stage 3: generate.

172
00:11:01,688 --> 00:11:07,977
To compare, Asha asks the AI for the same
function, and what it returns for milk.

173
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:12,217
The AI writes one line, and says: milk
gives 1.2.

174
00:11:12,820 --> 00:11:13,938
Look at line 7.

175
00:11:13,958 --> 00:11:16,366
It asks items for milk, by name.

176
00:11:16,386 --> 00:11:19,173
Remember what items is in the sample.

177
00:11:30,547 --> 00:11:30,763
No.

178
00:11:30,783 --> 00:11:31,786
A type error.

179
00:11:31,806 --> 00:11:37,687
Items is a list, and a list needs a number
as its index, not the name milk.

180
00:11:37,733 --> 00:11:40,969
That is what list indices must be integers
means.

181
00:11:40,989 --> 00:11:44,225
The AI treated the list as a dictionary of
names.

182
00:11:44,820 --> 00:11:45,894
Stage 4: test.

183
00:11:45,914 --> 00:11:51,050
Asha reports the error, and asks for test
cases with exact values.

184
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:54,207
The AI's next version loops over the list.

185
00:11:54,227 --> 00:11:58,078
And it lists three cases: milk, oat milk,
and flour.

186
00:11:58,687 --> 00:12:00,713
Line 9 returns the price.

187
00:12:00,733 --> 00:12:06,769
Line 20 prints each result with
exclamation r, so text shows its quotation

188
00:12:06,789 --> 00:12:07,260
marks.

189
00:12:18,613 --> 00:12:18,878
No.

190
00:12:18,898 --> 00:12:23,720
It got 1.20 in quotation marks: text, not
a number.

191
00:12:23,740 --> 00:12:27,233
Text is never equal to the float 1.2.

192
00:12:27,267 --> 00:12:28,678
Only flour passes.

193
00:12:28,698 --> 00:12:33,690
Plan said every price is text, and the AI
forgot its own words.

194
00:12:34,287 --> 00:12:38,447
Asha shows the AI the failing cases, and
asks why.

195
00:12:38,467 --> 00:12:43,801
The supplier sends prices as strings, so
the return line must convert them with

196
00:12:43,821 --> 00:12:44,208
float.

197
00:12:44,820 --> 00:12:47,242
She runs all three cases again.

198
00:12:47,262 --> 00:12:47,951
All pass.

199
00:12:48,553 --> 00:12:49,707
Stage 5: debug.

200
00:12:49,727 --> 00:12:56,047
The next day, oat milk is out of stock,
and the supplier sends null as its price.

201
00:12:56,067 --> 00:12:57,378
Milk still works.

202
00:12:57,398 --> 00:12:59,491
Oat milk crashes on line 9.

203
00:13:00,087 --> 00:13:03,457
Asha pastes the error, and asks for the
cause first.

204
00:13:03,500 --> 00:13:07,708
JSON null became None, and float of None
is a type error.

205
00:13:07,728 --> 00:13:11,269
The fix checks for it with is None, from
step 2.

206
00:13:11,887 --> 00:13:13,997
Lines 9 and 10 are the fix.

207
00:13:14,017 --> 00:13:19,914
She adds the new response as case 4, on
line 19, and runs every case again.

208
00:13:19,934 --> 00:13:21,019
All four pass.

209
00:13:21,620 --> 00:13:22,974
Stage 6: improve.

210
00:13:22,994 --> 00:13:28,630
Asha asks for one improvement, using only
field names from her sample.

211
00:13:28,667 --> 00:13:33,052
The AI suggests unit price, and calls it
the standard field.

212
00:13:33,653 --> 00:13:38,124
Asha's rule: every field name must appear
in the sample.

213
00:13:38,144 --> 00:13:40,609
Lines 9 and 11 read unit price.

214
00:13:51,980 --> 00:13:52,202
No.

215
00:13:52,222 --> 00:13:53,168
A key error.

216
00:13:53,188 --> 00:13:57,116
The sample has price and unit, but no unit
price.

217
00:13:57,136 --> 00:13:59,210
The AI invented the field.

218
00:13:59,233 --> 00:14:01,371
So Asha keeps her final function.

219
00:14:01,987 --> 00:14:06,088
She runs her four cases one more time, on
her own function.

220
00:14:06,108 --> 00:14:07,066
All four pass.

221
00:14:07,687 --> 00:14:08,520
Quick check.

222
00:14:08,540 --> 00:14:10,724
An AI's code uses a field name.

223
00:14:10,744 --> 00:14:12,147
How do you check it?

224
00:14:23,513 --> 00:14:23,628
B.

225
00:14:23,648 --> 00:14:25,642
Search the real sample for it.

226
00:14:25,662 --> 00:14:28,664
Only the sample shows what the service
sends.

227
00:14:29,287 --> 00:14:29,853
Breather.

228
00:14:29,873 --> 00:14:35,327
The AI made three confident mistakes, and
the sample and the tests caught every one.

229
00:14:35,367 --> 00:14:39,036
Next, four mistakes beginners make with
JSON.

230
00:14:43,783 --> 00:14:44,726
Mistake 1.

231
00:14:44,746 --> 00:14:45,497
You try.

232
00:14:45,517 --> 00:14:48,193
Line 4 holds some JSON text.

233
00:14:48,213 --> 00:14:49,733
Line 5 loads it.

234
00:15:01,113 --> 00:15:03,876
The text uses single quotation marks.

235
00:15:03,896 --> 00:15:08,463
Python accepts both kinds, but JSON
accepts only double ones.

236
00:15:08,500 --> 00:15:12,449
The report runs through the json module's
own files.

237
00:15:12,469 --> 00:15:16,343
On your computer, these lines show full
file paths.

238
00:15:16,363 --> 00:15:21,457
Skip to the last line: column 2 is the
first single quotation mark.

239
00:15:22,053 --> 00:15:22,810
Mistake 2.

240
00:15:22,830 --> 00:15:28,088
Dump writes into a file, so it needs one:
F P is the file parameter.

241
00:15:28,108 --> 00:15:30,261
To get a string, use dump s.

242
00:15:30,853 --> 00:15:31,779
Mistake 3.

243
00:15:31,799 --> 00:15:34,995
Lines 4 and 5 read scone dot json.

244
00:15:35,015 --> 00:15:37,359
Line 6 asks for the item.

245
00:15:48,713 --> 00:15:48,908
No.

246
00:15:48,928 --> 00:15:51,980
Read text gives a string, not a
dictionary.

247
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,623
A string accepts only number indexes.

248
00:15:54,667 --> 00:15:58,765
The fix: turn the text into data first,
with load s.

249
00:15:59,387 --> 00:16:00,154
Mistake 4.

250
00:16:00,174 --> 00:16:02,988
JSON object keys are always strings.

251
00:16:03,008 --> 00:16:07,397
The table numbers 1 and 2 came back as the
text 1 and 2.

252
00:16:07,433 --> 00:16:10,113
Asking back for key 1 is a key error.

253
00:16:10,133 --> 00:16:12,375
Use string keys from the start.

254
00:16:12,950 --> 00:16:13,312
First.

255
00:16:13,332 --> 00:16:17,964
Some weather services send the temperature
in Fahrenheit for some cities.

256
00:16:17,984 --> 00:16:21,278
How could your code know which unit a
response uses?

257
00:16:32,647 --> 00:16:35,123
Try it with the sample after the video.

258
00:16:35,720 --> 00:16:36,139
Second.

259
00:16:36,159 --> 00:16:40,274
Why test your code on a saved sample,
instead of the live service?

260
00:16:51,647 --> 00:16:52,663
Keep your answer.

261
00:16:52,683 --> 00:16:55,040
You will use it in the next boss level.

262
00:16:55,617 --> 00:16:56,506
Let's recap.

263
00:16:56,526 --> 00:17:00,977
Dump s makes JSON text, and load s turns
it back into data.

264
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,430
Dump and load work with an open file.

265
00:17:03,467 --> 00:17:07,387
True, false and null become True, False
and None.

266
00:17:07,433 --> 00:17:11,833
An API answers with a status code, and a
JSON body.

267
00:17:11,867 --> 00:17:16,307
Follow the path from the outside in, and
use get for missing fields.

268
00:17:16,333 --> 00:17:20,452
And check every field name an AI uses
against the real sample.

269
00:17:21,053 --> 00:17:22,338
One last check.

270
00:17:22,358 --> 00:17:26,512
A response says price, 1.20, in quotation
marks.

271
00:17:26,532 --> 00:17:29,033
What type is it after load s?

272
00:17:40,413 --> 00:17:40,545
C.

273
00:17:40,565 --> 00:17:41,227
A string.

274
00:17:41,247 --> 00:17:43,274
The quotation marks decide.

275
00:17:43,294 --> 00:17:46,533
Convert it with float before you
calculate.

276
00:17:47,153 --> 00:17:48,446
Mission complete!

277
00:17:48,466 --> 00:17:51,688
You earned 100 XP and the Connector badge.

278
00:17:53,917 --> 00:17:55,463
Now it is your turn.

279
00:17:55,483 --> 00:18:00,867
Three warm-up tasks worth 10 XP each, and
three apply tasks worth 20.

280
00:18:00,900 --> 00:18:06,397
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each,
including an AI's temperature reader to

281
00:18:06,417 --> 00:18:06,684
fix.

282
00:18:07,283 --> 00:18:11,485
Many programmers call web services with a
package called requests.

283
00:18:11,505 --> 00:18:14,683
It is not part of Python, so it must be
installed.

284
00:18:14,733 --> 00:18:20,338
In Chapter 24, you will create a virtual
environment, and install packages safely,

285
00:18:20,358 --> 00:18:23,973
after checking that each one is real and
trustworthy.

286
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:25,763
Do the warm-up tasks now.

287
00:18:25,783 --> 00:18:27,760
They take about ten minutes.

288
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:29,929
See you in Chapter 24.
